# =========================================================================================
# facility-profile.env - EVERY facility-specific value, in one place.
#
# This is the tailoring layer. The baseline documents in this directory are written to be
# reused as-is; this file is what changes per engagement.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT "just edit the documents": the same reason host-params.env exists.
# A value that appears in nine documents gets updated in seven of them, and the two that
# were missed are the ones an assessor reads. One source, referenced by placeholder.
#
# Placeholders in the baseline documents look like «SYSTEM_NAME» - guillemets, because they
# are visually obvious in prose and no one types them by accident. A document that still
# shows a guillemet has not been tailored. That is deliberate: a BLANK is safer than this
# lab's value silently standing in, because a wrong-but-plausible value carried into a
# second engagement is worse than an obvious gap.
#
#   grep -rn '«' docs/compliance/baseline/    # what still needs tailoring
#
# A PLACEHOLDER MUST NEVER CONTAIN A PIPE. The baseline documents are markdown, they are
# converted with pandoc, and most placeholders land inside a table cell - where a raw '|'
# ends the cell. Escaping it in the document (\|) then means the string in the document no
# longer matches the string in this file, so a find-and-replace tailoring pass silently
# misses it. Write alternatives as 'A or B', not 'A | B'.
#
# =========================================================================================

# --- the system, as the AO will refer to it ----------------------------------------------
SYSTEM_NAME='«SYSTEM_NAME»'                  # e.g. "Air-Gapped Container Platform Enclave"
SYSTEM_ACRONYM='«SYSTEM_ACRONYM»'
SYSTEM_ID='«SYSTEM_ID»'                      # eMASS / programme system identifier
SYSTEM_OWNER='«SYSTEM_OWNER»'                # the organisation, not a person
PPSM_ORG='«PPSM_ORG»'                        # ORG prefix on local CLSA entries, as the CAL's CLSA sheet uses it (DISA, DLA, DHA ...) - ppsm.py

# --- people. Roles, because a package is signed by roles ---------------------------------
AO_NAME='«AO_NAME»'                          # Authorizing Official
AODR_NAME='«AODR_NAME»'                      # AO Designated Representative, if one exists
ISSM_NAME='«ISSM_NAME»'
ISSO_NAME='«ISSO_NAME»'
SCA_NAME='«SCA_NAME»'                        # Security Control Assessor
SYSADMIN_NAME='«SYSADMIN_NAME»'
PREPARED_BY='«PREPARED_BY»'

# --- classification and impact -----------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTROL BASELINE IS THE SINGLE MOST EXPENSIVE VALUE IN THIS FILE. It determines which
# controls apply, and therefore how much of the SSP exists at all.
#
# IL5 commonly corresponds to a High-equivalent baseline plus DoD overlays for CUI, but
# "commonly" is not something to build a package on. Get it in writing; where the engagement
# will not say, write the baseline for High and record the alternative as a tailoring note
# rather than leaving it blank.
IMPACT_LEVEL='IL5'
CONFIDENTIALITY='«C_HIGH_MOD_LOW»'
INTEGRITY='«I_HIGH_MOD_LOW»'
AVAILABILITY='«A_HIGH_MOD_LOW»'
# DECIDED 2026-09-22 - PROVISIONAL, see the tailoring note below.
CONTROL_BASELINE='800-53 Rev 5 High'
OVERLAYS='CUI, NSS, Privacy'
# TAILORING NOTE, and it must travel with any document that cites these two values:
#   These are a PROVISIONAL determination recorded by the acting AO on 2026-09-22 so that the
#   package could proceed. They are NOT a determination by the engagement's accrediting
#   authority, and the real AO's answer supersedes them without argument.
#   Rationale for High: IL5 commonly corresponds to a High-equivalent baseline, and where an
#   engagement will not answer, this repository's own guidance (poam.md CUST-01,
#   nist-800-53-plan.md) is to write for High and record the alternative. Being wrong towards
#   High means REMOVING controls later; being wrong towards Moderate means an under-scoped SSP
#   that an assessor finds instead of us.
#   The alternative, recorded per that guidance: 800-53 Rev 5 MODERATE, which would reduce the
#   applicable control set materially and shrink ssp.md accordingly.
#   Overlays: CUI, NSS and Privacy are all claimed. NSS in particular EXPANDS scope beyond the
#   CUI-only case, and Privacy pulls in the privacy artifacts - so DATA_TYPES below stops being
#   optional. Revisit both the moment the real AO answers.
# ANSWERED 2026-09-22 by the acting AO. This value is load-bearing: it is why the Privacy
# overlay above is not a formality, and it is what makes D-7 a full PIA rather than a screening.
DATA_TYPES='Geospatial and map data (the dominant storage driver); PII of staff and operators; PII of members of the public; PHI; recorded human call records - who called, from where and about what - alongside API transaction volume'
# CONSEQUENCES, recorded so they are not rediscovered late:
#   - PII about members of the public means a PIA, not just a PTA. The PT family is fully in
#     scope in ssp.md and cannot be marked not-applicable.
#   - THE HEALTH DATA IS INCIDENTAL, decided 2026-09-22. This is NOT part of a DoD health care
#     programme and the data does not arrive from a covered provider or plan, so HIPAA and
#     DoDM 6025.18 do NOT apply and DoD's hybrid-entity access restriction is not engaged.
#     It is sensitive PII. REOPENS if the programme is ever designated a health care component,
#     or if health data begins arriving FROM one.
#   - A Privacy Act SORN EXISTS and covers this system. Its identifier is still needed, because
#     the PIA and the SSP both have to cite it and "there is one" is not a citation.
#   - EVERY COPY INHERITS IT. The off-host backup drive, the transfer SSD and any restore-test
#     artefact hold PII about members of the public, including health information. Removable
#     media custody, labelling, inventory and MP-6 sanitisation stop being lab conveniences.
#   - Access to PII records is expected to be auditable after the fact (PT family, AU-2/AU-12).
#     The enclave's audit trail is 40 MB rolling with NO OFFLOAD - see the audit-offload thread.
#   - CALL RECORDS ARE METADATA ONLY, no audio retained. So "call volume is light" stands as a
#     sizing statement and map storage remains the dominant driver.

# --- where it physically is ---------------------------------------------------------------
# PE controls are normally INHERITED from the facility's existing ATO. Name the ATO being
# inherited from; "the facility is secure" is not an inheritance statement.
FACILITY_NAME='«FACILITY_NAME»'
FACILITY_LOCATION='«CITY, STATE»'
FACILITY_ATO_REF='«the ATO PE controls are inherited from, or NONE»'

# --- the boundary -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The strongest sentence in this package. Answer it deliberately per site: in this lab the
# enclave has NO external connections at all, and the staging machines are outside the
# boundary and physically disconnected at cutover.
EXTERNAL_CONNECTIONS='«NONE or list each, with an ISA/MOU reference»'
BOUNDARY_ROUTER_IN_SCOPE='«yes or no - inherited»'

# --- artifacts this AO actually wants ----------------------------------------------------
# Varies by Service, programme and eMASS configuration. Record what was ASKED FOR, so the
# next engagement can see what differed rather than re-deriving it.
ARTIFACTS_REQUESTED='«paste the list the AO provided»'
TRADITIONAL_SECURITY_CHECKLIST='«required or inherited or not applicable»'
IR_PLAN='«system-specific or inherited from the programme»'
CONTINUOUS_MONITORING_REPORTING='«to whom, how often, in what format»'

# --- dates --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ATO_TARGET_DATE='«YYYY-MM-DD»'
ASSESSMENT_WINDOW='«YYYY-MM-DD to YYYY-MM-DD»'
DOC_VERSION='0.1 draft'
DOC_DATE='«YYYY-MM-DD»'

# --- hardware, per site -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The LAB values live in scripts/enclave/vm-specs.env and runbook §1.5. They are test
# hardware and MUST NOT be quoted as a production recommendation - the runbook says so
# explicitly. Production sizing depends on the application storage requirement, which is the
# same uncaptured input that blocks the Ceph OSD size and therefore the BOM.
HW_PROFILE='«lab or production - see runbook 1.5 and the BOM»'
APP_STORAGE_REQUIREMENT='«TB of usable persistent storage - UNANSWERED in the lab»'

# =========================================================================================
# POA&M  (poam.md)
# =========================================================================================
# Every POA&M row carries its OWN owner and completion date. These two are the placeholder
# TOKENS the baseline register prints in those columns - they are not one global answer.
# Tailoring means replacing them row by row, which is the point: assembling the POA&M is
# what forces every open item in this build to acquire a name and a date.
POAM_ROW_OWNER='«OWNER»'
POAM_ROW_TARGET='«TARGET_DATE»'

# Who signs a risk acceptance when a row will never be remediated. Usually the AO or AODR;
# some programmes delegate low-risk acceptances to the ISSM. Get it in writing.
POAM_ACCEPTANCE_AUTHORITY='«who signs a risk acceptance»'

# eMASS, Xacta, a spreadsheet on a programme share - it changes the format, not the content.
POAM_SUBMISSION_SYSTEM='«eMASS or Xacta or other»'
POAM_REVIEW_CADENCE='«how often the POA&M is reviewed and re-submitted»'

# =========================================================================================
# Configuration Management Plan  (cm-plan.md)
# =========================================================================================
# The CM authority. This build's technical baseline is an executable procedure in a git
# repository; what does NOT exist is the body that approves a change to it.
CM_AUTHORITY='«CCB name, or the individual who approves changes»'
CM_AUTHORITY_CADENCE='«how often it meets, or "by ticket" if there is no board»'
CM_CHANGE_REQUEST_SYSTEM='«Jira or ServiceNow or eMASS or email to the ISSM»'

# Where the repository of record lives for the customer, and who holds it. In this build it
# is a git repository on the staging machine plus a public origin with the client-private
# paths withheld; a programme normally wants it somewhere they control.
CM_REPO_OF_RECORD='«the authoritative repository location and its custodian»'

# Who may authorise material crossing the air gap, and on what evidence.
CM_MEDIA_TRANSFER_AUTHORITY='«who approves a transfer across the gap»'

# The maintenance window a patch cycle is allowed to consume. Note that patching the
# hypervisor is an enclave outage (runbook 10c) and a full backup costs 2x the data in I/O
# (runbook 10b.1) - size the window against those two numbers, not against apt.
CM_PATCH_WINDOW='«day/time and duration available for patching»'
CM_PATCH_CADENCE='«how often packages are carried in and applied»'

# =========================================================================================
# Contingency Plan  (contingency-plan.md)
# =========================================================================================
# THE TWO MOST EXPENSIVE VALUES IN THIS FILE AFTER THE CONTROL BASELINE. They are
# COMMITMENTS, not measurements, and nothing in this build has committed to them. Do not
# fill them in from what the lab happens to achieve - measure first, then commit, then
# design to the commitment.
CP_RTO='«Recovery Time Objective - NOT YET COMMITTED»'
CP_RPO='«Recovery Point Objective - NOT YET COMMITTED»'
CP_MTD='«Maximum Tolerable Downtime»'
CP_ESSENTIAL_FUNCTIONS='«the mission functions the enclave must restore first»'

# Recovery roles. A one-operator enclave has a single point of human failure, and an
# assessor will ask who the second person is.
CP_RECOVERY_LEAD='«recovery lead»'
CP_RECOVERY_ALTERNATE='«alternate, and how they are reached»'
CP_NOTIFICATION_LIST='«who is notified of an outage, and by what means - NOT email from inside the gap»'

# The LUKS passphrase and the GRUB superuser password are boot-time secrets held in a
# controlled document, never in the repository. Recovery from a power event requires a human
# who holds them to be physically at the rack.
CP_BOOT_SECRET_CUSTODIAN='«who holds the LUKS passphrase and the GRUB password, and where»'

# Backup media and storage. In the lab the destination is an encrypted USB volume attached
# to the same machine that runs the guests, which is not a backup posture - it is a
# parameter with a bench value in it.
CP_BACKUP_DESTINATION='«production backup destination - iSCSI/NFS/LUN, NOT a USB disk on the hypervisor»'
CP_BACKUP_OFFSITE='«offsite or off-host copy location, or NONE»'
CP_BACKUP_MEDIA_CUSTODIAN='«who handles backup media leaving the room, under what procedure»'
CP_BACKUP_RETENTION='«how many chains, how long, and where the requirement comes from»'

# Alternate site. This design is single-site with no standby. If the programme requires a
# recovery site, that is a second enclave and a second ATO boundary - not a configuration
# change.
CP_ALTERNATE_SITE='«NONE or the alternate processing site and its agreement reference»'
CP_ALTERNATE_STORAGE='«NONE or the alternate storage site for backup media»'

# Testing. CP-4 has NO evidence in this build - no test of any kind has been performed.
CP_TEST_CADENCE='«how often the plan is exercised»'
CP_TEST_TYPE='«tabletop or functional or full failover»'
CP_LAST_TEST_DATE='«NONE - no contingency test has ever been run»'

# =========================================================================================
# ISCM Strategy  (iscm-strategy.md)
# =========================================================================================
# CONTINUOUS_MONITORING_REPORTING above says to whom and how often. These are the values
# the running stack needs that only the programme can supply.
ISCM_REPORT_RECIPIENT='«who receives the monitoring report»'
ISCM_REPORT_CADENCE='«monthly or quarterly»'
ISCM_REPORT_FORMAT='«the format required - eMASS entry, PDF, dashboard screenshot»'

# The notification answer. Two STIG controls require email notification and no email can
# leave an air gap (open-questions Q26). Until this is answered, an alert is something a
# human has to look at a screen to see.
ISCM_NOTIFICATION_DECISION='«the AO answer on whether an in-boundary dashboard alert satisfies notification»'
ISCM_ALERT_REVIEW_OWNER='«who reviews Alertmanager, and how often»'
ISCM_ALERT_REVIEW_CADENCE='«how often the alert console is actually looked at»'

# Assessment and scan frequency the AO expects. The tooling runs on demand today.
ISCM_STIG_SCAN_CADENCE='«how often each machine is re-scanned»'
ISCM_ASSESSOR_INDEPENDENCE='«who performs the independent assessment - self-assessment is a different artifact»'

# Retention. Both of these are AO answers that size disk.
ISCM_AUDIT_RETENTION_ONLINE='«how long audit records stay online»'
ISCM_AUDIT_RETENTION_ARCHIVE='«how long they are archived, and where»'
ISCM_METRIC_RETENTION='«Prometheus retention - 90d/100GB is a placeholder, not a decision»'

# The acceptable age of vulnerability data, which sets the transfer cadence for Trivy's
# database (open-questions Q27).
ISCM_MAX_VULN_DATA_AGE='«at what age does a scan against carried-in data stop being acceptable»'

# Does the programme already run a SIEM or STIG Manager this enclave must feed? If yes, the
# only routes out of the boundary are sneakernet or a one-way transfer device.
ISCM_EXTERNAL_SIEM='«NONE or the SIEM this must feed, and by what transfer mechanism»'
ISCM_STIG_MANAGER='«NONE or the programme runs STIG Manager and expects CKLs fed to it»'

# =========================================================================================
# STORAGE SIZING - per site, and DO NOT carry this lab's numbers forward
# =========================================================================================
# Learned 2026-09-17: the three lab hosts came up with 953.9 GB, 931.5 GB and 1.8 TB data
# disks because that is what was on the shelf. The RULE is architectural - every Ceph OSD in
# the cluster is the same size, and that size is DECLARED, set to the smallest data-disk
# remainder across all hosts (runbook 9a.2). The NUMBER is per site and belongs here.
#
# Nothing is lost by evening them: replica-3 across four nodes is gated by the smallest OSDs,
# so capacity above that floor was never usable - an oversized OSD only buys a hotspot.
OSD_SIZE_PER_NODE='«e.g. 431G - the smallest data-disk remainder across all hosts»'
CEPH_USABLE_ESTIMATE='«4 x OSD_SIZE / 3 at replica-3, then 60% for the working figure»'

# How much of each data disk becomes the PostgreSQL guest volume, before the OSD gets the rest.
# This is DATA_VG_SIZE in host-params.env; recorded here so the sizing rationale lives in one
# place at tailoring time.
PG_DATA_VG_SIZE='«e.g. 500G»'

# WHERE THE WAL ARCHIVE LIVES. The REQUIREMENT is architectural and non-negotiable: it must NOT
# be on the same machine as the whole-VM backup volume, or the two recovery paths are one
# (runbook 9a.1 correction 1). The LOCATION is a facility decision.
#
# In the lab, host-3's 1.8 TB disk left ~900 GB that Ceph cannot use, so the archive goes there
# for free. THAT IS AN ACCIDENT OF THIS HARDWARE. A site with three identical drives has no
# spare and must meet the requirement another way - a dedicated volume, a customer-provided NFS
# or iSCSI target, or a further machine.
WAL_ARCHIVE_LOCATION='«where, and on which machine - must differ from the backup volume host»'
WAL_ARCHIVE_TOOL='«pgbackrest recommended - it handles a full destination correctly; a bare cp does not»'

# ---- ACCOUNTS (backlog 3.15) - read by stig-tailor.sh accounts AND answerfile.sh --------
# The second named administrator is a PERSON, so it is a site fact, not a script default.
# One place, so the machines and the answer file cannot disagree: before 2026-09-24 the
# answer file got this name only when someone typed AF_ADMINS=... by hand, and a rebuild
# with the default would have re-opened V-270682 and V-270748.
ADMIN2_USER='bmcvey'                         # lab value; a site's second administrator
BREAKGLASS_USER='breakglass'                 # the console-only emergency account
# The second admin's SSH PUBLIC key (not secret). Either the key text itself, or - when the
# second admin uses the same key as the reference admin - the COMMENT of that key's line in
# the reference admin's authorized_keys. The comment match is what stops the automation key
# being copied to a person. ADMIN2_PUBKEY wins when both are set.
ADMIN2_PUBKEY=''
ADMIN2_KEY_COMMENT='enclave-admin'           # matched against the LAST word of each key's comment
